<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pannonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pannonia is a historical region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, covering parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, originally a Roman province. In the late eighth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> campaigned there against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>. Its conquest extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>'s south-eastern frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pannonia.jpg" alt="Pannonia" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pannonia01b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 568 the Lombards left <b>Pannonia</b> for Italy, and the Avars took over the whole basin, the great plain enclosed by the Carpathians and the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…than thirty years of brutal campaigning, mass deportations and forced Christianisation; and the Avar campaigns of the 790s destroyed the power of the Avar khaganate in <b>Pannonia</b> and brought back treasure so vast — reportedly fifteen wagons, each drawn by four oxen — that it transformed Carolingian finances.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first named ruler is Mojmír I, “duke of the Moravians”, who according to the Conversio Bagoariorum et Carantanorum expelled Pribina across the Danube; Pribina fled to Ratpot, who administered the March of <b>Pannonia</b> from around 833.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ellak, his favoured son and ruler of the Akatziri, met the Gepid king Ardaric's coalition at the Battle of Nedao, around 454, and the Amali Goths under Valamir revolted the same year; many Germanic vassals were lost and the Huns were driven from <b>Pannonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger Beck lists the places of the earliest phase (c. 80–120 CE): Rome, Nida and Mogontiacum in Germania, Pons Aeni in Noricum, Carnuntum in <b>Pannonia</b>, Novae and Oescus in Moesia, and Caesarea Maritima in Judaea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…Luke found at Arsinoë in Egypt in 1907 and destroyed by water damage in 1945, after it had been copied; four leaves of Romans at Wolfenbüttel; a lead plate from <b>Pannonia</b> with verses of the gospels; a calendar, glosses, and two deeds on papyrus from Naples and Arezzo.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
